<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Westminster Abbey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Westminster Abbey is a church in Westminster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> and consecrated in 1065, then rebuilt again in Gothic style from 1245 under Henry III. Every English and British monarch since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, crowned there on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day 1066, has been crowned in the abbey, except Edward V and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-viii">Edward VIII</a>, who were never crowned. Many kings, queens and notable figures are buried there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey.jpg" alt="Westminster Abbey" width="320" height="643" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antiquary · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Westminster_Abbey,_Westminster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Formally the Collegiate Church of Saint Peter at Westminster, the abbey has obscure beginnings. Historians agree that a monastery dedicated to Saint Peter stood on the site before the 11th century, and an abbey housing Benedictine monks was there by the mid-10th century. Legend credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeberht-of-essex">Sæberht of Essex</a> or a fictional 2nd-century king, Lucius, with founding it, and a tradition tells of a fisherman on the Thames who had a vision of Saint Peter; the Fishmongers' Company still gives the abbey a salmon each year in memory of it.</p><p>Between 1042 and 1052 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> began rebuilding it as a royal burial church. It was Romanesque and the first church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on a cruciform plan, with Leofsi Duddason as master stonemason. The community grew from a dozen monks to as many as 80. Edward died about a week after the consecration, and was buried in the church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Henry III began the present church on 6 July 1245 under his master mason Henry of Reynes, intending a shrine for Edward and a burial place for his own family, to match great French churches such as Reims Cathedral. It was designed with long transepts to hold many guests at coronations. Abbot Nicholas Litlyngton and Richard II paid for the completion of the nave from 1376, and the mason Henry Yevele followed the original design even though it was more than a century out of date.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> seized the monastery in the 1530s and his agents removed relics and treasures, melting down the golden feretory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>. Elizabeth I dissolved the monastery in 1559 and in 1560 chartered the church as a royal peculiar, answerable directly to the sovereign. Early in the 17th century the Jerusalem Chamber hosted meetings of translators of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">King James Version</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, and the abbey's western towers, by Nicholas Hawksmoor and John James, were built in the 1740s. In June 1914 a suffragette bomb damaged the Coronation Chair.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The church is chiefly Geometric Gothic, with an eleven-bay nave and a vault 102 feet (31 m) high. The Henry VII Chapel, in late Perpendicular Gothic, was called orbis miraculum, the wonder of the world, by John Leland. Coronations take place on the Cosmati pavement before the shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>. Over 3,300 people are buried or commemorated there, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-chaucer">Geoffrey Chaucer</a>, whose burial began Poets' Corner, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>, whose lavish funeral in 1727 boosted the honour of burial there. The Unknown Warrior was buried in the nave on 11 November 1920. The abbey, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-westminster">Palace of Westminster</a> and St Margaret's Church form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, listed in 1987.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Westminster Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 1740s it was held in the chapter house of <b>Westminster Abbey</b>, and in 1859 it moved to the new Public Record Office.</blockquote><a href="/work/domesday-book">Domesday Book · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke William of Normandy defeated and killed Harold Godwinson at the Battle of Hastings on 14 October 1066, and on Christmas Day he was crowned king of England in <b>Westminster Abbey</b>, the church rebuilt by Edward the Confessor and consecrated only the year before.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>It was sung at the burial of the Unknown Warrior in <b>Westminster Abbey</b> in 1920, and is sometimes sung on Anzac Day in Australia and New Zealand and on Remembrance Day in Canada and the United Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/songs/abide-with-me">Abide with Me</a></li><li><blockquote>Some Church of England clergy hold that it is not strictly a hymn, since it is not a prayer to God, yet it is sung in many churches, on Saint George's Day, at the 2011 royal wedding in <b>Westminster Abbey</b> and in schools across Britain and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/songs/and-did-those-feet-in-ancient-time">Jerusalem (And did those feet in ancient time)</a></li><li><blockquote>William and Mary were crowned together in <b>Westminster Abbey</b> after accepting the Declaration of Rights.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry II, the first Plantagenet king, was crowned in <b>Westminster Abbey</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-19">19 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
